Use CSS `:hover` on the `span` itself. The `:hover` pseudo-class works on *all elements*, not just links, and it matches when the pointer is over the element.[6][7]

```html
<span class="my-span">Hover me</span>
```

```css
.my-span {
  color: black;
}

.my-span:hover {
  color: red;
}
```

If you want a smoother change, add a transition:

```css
.my-span {
  color: black;
  transition: color 0.2s ease;
}

.my-span:hover {
  color: red;
}
```

If the `span` contains other styled parts or inline styles are overriding your rule, you may need to increase specificity or remove the inline styling; inline styles can override stylesheet rules unless you change that setup.[1]

